    21	`GET /health` confirms the Worker/API version. `GET /v1/operations/snapshot` reports active/expired inboxes, pending/failed parses, storage estimate, webhook failures, authorization failures, and rate-limit events with threshold alerts. Usage records support tenant billing/reconciliation but do not activate a billing provider.
    22	
    23	Alert on Queue DLQ growth, `queue_failed` or `parse_failed` messages, webhook quarantine, authorization/rate-limit spikes, R2/D1 errors, retention deletion failures, stored-byte growth, and Email Routing failures. Cloudflare logs must not capture Authorization headers or content-bearing bodies.
    24	
    25	Internal rollout succeeds only after a seven-day observation window meets all of these thresholds: at least 99.9% of accepted messages reach `ready` without operator repair, parse latency p95 is under 30 seconds, wait-to-visible latency p95 is under 35 seconds, the committed MIME extraction fixture set remains 100% deterministic, attachment/raw hash mismatches are zero, orphaned R2 objects found by reconciliation are zero, DLQ rate is below 0.1%, and MailFn-specific operational work is below one intervention per 1,000 accepted messages. Any threshold breach pauses expansion and triggers rollback to the prior Worker route.
    26	
    27	## Recovery
    28	
    29	- Queue outage: accepted messages remain `queue_failed` with raw MIME. Call `POST /v1/admin/reconcile` after Queue recovery.
    30	- Parse failure: raw MIME remains. Fix parser/configuration and replay the versioned job; consumers must tolerate at-least-once delivery.
    31	- Webhook outage: delivery state remains independent of message acceptance. Retry bounded transient failures; quarantine endpoints after repeated failures.
    32	- Retention deletion failure: message/attachment metadata remains so the next scheduled pass can retry. Review `retention.delete_failed` audits.
    33	- D1 metadata write after R2 raw write: raw cleanup is best effort. Inventory R2 keys without matching D1 IDs during maintenance.
    34	- R2 loss: normalized D1 message remains but raw/attachment retrieval fails; restore from bucket recovery if configured.
    35	- Project/inbox bootstrap: D1 batch transactions commit the resource and its initial credential/idempotency record together; a batch failure exposes neither.
    36	- Concurrent inbound duplicate: the unique D1 delivery constraint elects one canonical message; the losing request removes its own R2 object and quota reservations, then returns the canonical row.
    37	- Custom-domain activation: if D1 cannot persist the active rule ID, MailFn deletes the newly created provider rule. Rule creation first reuses a matching owned rule to make retry safe.
    38	- Outbound failure: a newly created daily quota reservation is released when SendFn rejects before accepting the send; queued/sent provider acceptance keeps one deterministic per-draft usage record.
